{"slides":[{"k":"title","h":"WHICH route to market?","sub":"Session 2 · Live class · One supplier, a pool of suppliers, or a negotiation","note":"Welcome everyone back; check sound, cameras and that the workbook opens. Remind them: in Session 1 we decided WHAT we buy and WHO holds the power. Today we turn that into a decision: how each category goes to market. They have watched the lessons, so this is not a lecture. Today is for arguing, testing and deciding. Say the message of the day once, up front, and tell them you will repeat it until they are tired of it: cleaning and security go to one supplier tendered about every three years; professional and IT services go to a pool of preferred suppliers with a rate card and mini-competitions; incumbent software is a prepared negotiation, not a tender. Two minutes, then the plan."},{"k":"table","h":"Today's plan","t":[["Time","Block","How"],["09:00","Opening and Fieldwork 1 debrief","Poll and trios"],["09:15","2.1 The six decisions of a sourcing strategy","Debate, practice on your project"],["09:55","2.2 One supplier, chosen by tender","Dilemma, poll, Nordvia cleaning design"],["10:30","Break",""],["10:40","2.3 A pool of preferred suppliers","Debate, panel design in breakout rooms"],["11:25","2.4 When there is no real competition","Dilemma, poll, Kelvar outline"],["11:55","Break",""],["12:05","2.5 Partnerships, the tail and the strategy paper","Write your one-pager"],["12:30","Case 2: four projects, four routes","Team exercise and debrief"],["12:55","Close and Fieldwork 2",""]],"note":"Walk through the plan in under a minute. Point out the two breaks (10:30 and 11:55). Say that 2.3 is the longest block on purpose: professional and IT services are where most of our indirect money and most of our bad habits sit (in the Group, IT services alone are about €80M with some 350 suppliers). Say the output of the day: the first version of their one-page sourcing strategy paper, which they take to the business owner in Fieldwork 2. Ask them to keep the chat open all morning: every debate starts there."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s2-l1","note":"Launch at once, 60 seconds, as an energiser. Read the result without judging. A good sign is that some scopes narrowed, grew or changed shape: that is exactly why we validate before choosing a route. If several people did not hold the conversation, do not shame them; say that today gives them a much better reason to book it, because they will bring a decision, not a questionnaire. Pick two volunteers for the next slide: one whose scope changed and one whose need turned out to be something else."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Fieldwork 1: what did the business owner really say?","q":"Name one thing your business owner confirmed, and one thing they changed.","b":["Is the need real, or a habit?","The contract fact that surprised you: expiry, notice period, auto-renewal","A triage flag: outsourcing, DORA, personal data, staff transfer","The question you still could not answer"],"min":12,"format":"Trios, then two stories in plenary","note":"Trios for 6 minutes, then the two volunteers from the poll tell their story in one minute each, then one or two more from the floor. Listen for, and write on the whiteboard: (1) notice dates nobody knew, (2) auto-renewal clauses, (3) triage flags (DORA, outsourcing, staff transfer), (4) favourite suppliers. These four come back all morning. Pitfall: people retell the whole project. Cut them kindly: 'what changed after the conversation?' Bridge: Nordvia's team of nine ran about 60 events a year, most of them RFQs with three quotes whatever the category. Ask: does that sound like your team? Then move on."},{"k":"section","n":"2.1","h":"The six decisions of a sourcing strategy","sub":"Strategy first, tool last. The mechanism is decision five, not decision one.","note":"Short bridge, two minutes. The lesson covered the six decisions (number of suppliers, relationship, contract architecture, commercial model, competitive mechanism, term) and the strategy-selection matrix. We will not repeat them. Ask one question to warm up: 'In your last sourcing event, what did you decide first?' Most honest answers will be 'the RFQ' or 'which suppliers to invite'. That is the habit we are breaking today: the tool follows the strategy, and the strategy follows the power position (CIPS strategy stage, O'Brien's 5i, Kraljic and the Chessboard from Session 1)."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Three quotes for everything?","q":"\"Three quotes for every purchase is the fairest and safest rule Procurement can have.\"","b":["What does it do to cleaning: three sites, three local cleaners, rolled over since 2019?","What does it do to 38 consulting call-offs a year?","What does it do to Kelvar, where only one vendor can really quote?","If not a rule, what should decide the route?"],"min":12,"format":"Agree / disagree, then open floor","note":"Ask for thumbs up (agree) or down (disagree) in reactions. Expect a split: many teams live by this rule because auditors like it. Call one person from each side. Push the agree side with the three Nordvia cases: three quotes per building every year means three cleaners rotating through HQ; three quotes per consulting job means 38 mini-tenders or, in practice, people skip it and pick a favourite (29 of 38 call-offs had no competition); three quotes for Kelvar means two quotes for show. Push the disagree side: what replaces the rule so it is still auditable? Land it: the route is decided by how demand arrives and whether there is a real alternative, and it is written down with the options rejected. That is the strategy paper. Twelve minutes, no more."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Practice 2.1: the six decisions for your project","min":15,"where":"Alone · Module 2.1 in the workbook","b":["Bring forward your Kraljic and Chessboard position from Session 1","Say how demand arrives: one service, a stream of jobs, one-off, many small orders","Answer the six decisions, in order","Fill the 'option rejected' column: that is where the thinking shows"],"note":"Individual work, cameras optional, 15 minutes in the main room. Post the four steps in the chat. Walk the room by reading answers in the workbook if you can see them, otherwise ask two people to share screen briefly at minute 10. What to look for: (1) people who filled decision 5 (mechanism) first, (2) an empty 'option rejected' column, (3) a term chosen without reference to switching cost. Expected pattern: site services land on one supplier and a 3 to 5-year term; consulting and IT services on several suppliers and a framework; software renewals on sole source. At minute 12 give a three-minute warning. Then debrief on the next slide."},{"k":"table","h":"2.1 recap: where does the competition happen?","t":[["Pattern","Competition happens","Typical categories","Main tool"],["One supplier by tender","For the contract, about every three years","Cleaning, security, soft FM","RFP, quality at least 50%"],["Pool of preferred suppliers","Within the panel, job by job","Consulting, IT services, agencies","Framework with rate card, mini-competitions"],["Sole- or single-source","Not in the contract horizon: negotiate","Incumbent core software","Usage facts, benchmark, BATNA"],["Around them","RFQ and e-auction; catalogues; multi-stage","Precise buys; tail; transformations","Sessions 3 and 4 go deeper"]],"note":"Debrief the practice first, five minutes: ask 'who changed their mind on decision 1 while filling the table?' and 'who has a rejected option the business owner would have chosen?' (that is the objection they will meet in Fieldwork 2). Then use this table as the recap of 2.1 and the spine of the day. Read the 'Competition happens' column slowly: FOR the contract, WITHIN the panel, or NOT AT ALL in the horizon. Recall the five route-to-market questions in order: is there a real alternative in the horizon; how does demand arrive; can we specify precisely and rank automatically; is the solution itself unknown; does a Group or entity framework already cover it? Ask each participant to type in the chat which row their own project is in. Keep the answers: we test them again at the end."},{"k":"section","n":"2.2","h":"One supplier, chosen by tender","sub":"Cleaning, security, soft FM: the competition happens once per cycle. Make that moment count.","note":"Bridge in one minute: the first pattern. Recall the Nordvia facts from the lesson: P1 office cleaning, 3 sites in 2 countries, €2.4M a year, three local cleaners rolled over since 2019, quality complaints at HQ, expiry June 2027. P6 security and reception, €1.6M, one provider, hourly rates indexed twice in two years, no KPIs. Ask: who in the room has a site service that has been rolled over more than once? Usually many hands. That is the starting point for the dilemma."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Why not a panel of cleaners?","q":"\"Consultants get a panel. So why not give HQ cleaning to three cleaners and let them compete every month?\"","b":["Who is accountable when the third floor is dirty?","What does each change of cleaner cost: recruitment, training, staff transfer?","Where does the competition really happen in cleaning?","And the opposite: why not one cleaner for the whole Group?"],"min":8,"format":"Two camps, one minute each","note":"Name two camps: half the room defends the panel of cleaners, half defends one supplier. One minute each, then two minutes open. Expected arguments for one supplier: accountability for a building; labour is most of the cost, so bundled hours give density and better price; every change costs mobilisation and possibly a staff transfer under the Acquired Rights Directive and national rules (Ellram's post-transaction costs); one set of KPIs and one review. The panel camp will say 'tension'. Answer: the tension is in the tender, for the whole contract, and in the options that must be earned. Then the reverse provocation: one cleaner for the whole Group? Good answers: labour markets and collective agreements are local, so one supplier per site or cluster (per country), not one for ten entities. Land the line: competition FOR the contract, not WITHIN it."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s2-l2","note":"Run it in 45 seconds. Expected majority: one supplier per lot for 3 + 1 + 1. Read the minority answers aloud, they are the useful ones. 'Two cleaners per site' feels safe but doubles supervision and splits accountability. 'Yearly RFQ, lowest price' is how bids fall below the wage floor: the bid that looks cheapest is often a future service failure or a legal exposure with our name on it. 'Roll over again' is what Nordvia has done since 2019. Remind them 3 + 1 + 1 is sound practice, not a norm: public examples include 3 + 2 × 12 months for a UK university cleaning contract (50/50 quality and price, 2024), and HMRC integrated FM at 5 + 3 + 1 + 1. Then open the practice."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Practice 2.2: the Nordvia cleaning re-tender","min":15,"where":"Pairs in breakout rooms · Module 2.2 in the workbook","b":["Lots: one lot, one per country, or one per site?","Term and options","Specification type and quality/price weights","Safeguards, and the timeline back from June 2027","One line: what you would adapt for your own site service"],"note":"Pairs, 15 minutes. Post the brief in the chat before you open the rooms. Visit three or four rooms for a minute each. Expected answer (facilitator guide): one lot per country, because collective agreements and markets differ, with a bundle option for both; 3 + 1 + 1; hybrid specification (outputs for most areas, prescribed methods where we need control, such as data rooms or security); quality at least 50%, fixed before issue; a should-cost floor (hours × collective wage + on-costs, supervision, consumables, overhead, margin); staff transfer data in the RFP; wage-index indexation; KPIs with service credits. Accept one lot for all three sites if they argue for one provider, but ask how they handle two labour regimes. Push back on any price-only auction. Five-minute and one-minute warnings."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Debrief: is 3 + 1 + 1 a decision or a habit?","q":"Your lots, term and weights: which choice would the Head of Real Estate challenge first?","b":["One lot, one per country, or one per site: why?","Who earns the options, and how do we measure it?","Hybrid specification: where did you prescribe the method?","June 2027 minus 9 to 12 months of tender: is there still time? What is plan B?"],"min":6,"format":"Room spokespeople, then open floor","note":"Ask three pairs for their lot structure and term in one sentence each. Then go straight to the timing question, which is the real insight: a standard tender needs about 9 to 12 months before expiry, plus mobilisation, so P1 is already tight. A short bridge extension of the current contracts is defensible only if it is a documented decision with a date, not another roll-over. If someone mentions an e-auction, ask: on what? If price alone decides cleaning, bids drop below the wage floor; at most an auction among bidders above a quality threshold and the should-cost floor. Session 3 evaluates real Nordvia cleaning bids, so do not open that now."},{"k":"takeaways","h":"2.2 in five lines","b":["Competition happens for the contract, once per cycle: make it count","One supplier per site or cluster: accountability, density, lower mobilisation cost","3 + 1 + 1 is a sound default, with options earned by performance (practice, not a norm)","Hybrid specification, quality at least 50%, a should-cost floor, staff transfer data","Never a price-only auction for cleaning or security"],"note":"Read the five lines in one minute. Add P6 in one sentence: security and reception follow the same logic, with more weight on vetting, licences and prescribed procedures. Ask for one word in the chat: which of these lines is missing from your entity's last site-service tender? Then announce the break and the question on the next slide."},{"k":"statement","h":"Break · 10 minutes","p":"Back at 10:40. Think: which of your current suppliers would you put on a panel, and which would you never?","note":"Put a visible timer on screen. Leave the question visible so people come back with an answer: it sets up 2.3. Use the break to scan the chat for the 'which row is my project in' answers from 2.1 and note any that look wrong, for a private word later. Restart on time even if a few are missing."},{"k":"section","n":"2.3","h":"A pool of preferred suppliers","sub":"Professional and IT services arrive as a stream of different jobs. No single firm is best at all of them.","note":"Restart with two answers to the break question. Then the facts, briefly: Nordvia P2 IT consulting, €6.1M with 12 firms through 38 call-offs; the top 3 take 61%; 29 call-offs without competition; 820 to 1,450 euros a day for the same senior profile. The Group picture (anonymised): IT services about €80M with some 350 suppliers; advisory firms about €12M in 9 entities through 60 supplier records and no Group agreement. Ask: is that a market, or a habit? Then the provocation."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Why not one consulting firm for everything?","q":"\"Give all IT consulting to one big firm: one contract, one discount, one relationship manager. Done.\"","b":["Is any firm best at architecture, testing, actuarial and DORA work at once?","What happens to price tension after year one?","What do 29 of 38 call-offs without competition tell us about today?","And the opposite: why not an open RFP for every assignment?"],"min":10,"format":"Agree / disagree","note":"Reactions first, then one voice from each side. The one-firm camp has a real point: fewer suppliers per unit of spend is associated with top performance in Hackett's benchmarks (a consultancy benchmark, not peer-reviewed). Accept it, then ask: fewer, or one? One firm gives dependency, capability gaps and no tension per job. Then the opposite camp: an open RFP per job takes weeks, so people skip it and pick a favourite, which is how we got 29 call-offs without competition. Land the answer: a pool of preferred suppliers by lot, with maximum day rates fixed once, and competition between three to six pre-qualified firms per job. Mention the analogue: Zurich cut its England and Wales claims legal panel from 28 to 14 firms (2004), citing one central system; a claims-legal panel, not indirect, but the logic transfers."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s2-l3","note":"Run it in 30 seconds. Expected majority: 3 to 6. Say clearly that three to six per lot is common practice, not a verified norm. Then test the edges. '1' means no competition per job: that is the one-firm answer again. 'All 12' or 'anyone who passes onboarding' means members get little work, stop putting their best people forward and stop investing in us. The test to give them: can each member expect meaningful work in a year? Size the panel to the demand of the lot, not to keep everyone happy. Then open the breakout rooms."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Practice 2.3: design the Nordvia IT services panel","min":20,"where":"Breakout rooms of 3–4 · Module 2.3 in the workbook","b":["Check the two numbers: call-offs without competition, rate spread","Lots by capability and firms per lot","Rate card structure: illustrative maximum day rates by role and seniority","Call-off rules: below €50K, €50K–250K, above €250K, urgent, follow-on","Labour-law and DORA safeguards for staff augmentation"],"note":"Rooms of three or four, 20 minutes. Post the brief in the chat first. Check answers are validated in the workbook: 76% of call-offs without competition (29 of 38); the top day rate is 77% above the lowest ((1,450 − 820) ÷ 820). A good panel: two or three lots (IT delivery, technology advisory, IT staff augmentation; regulatory and actuarial as a possible fourth), three to five firms per lot, maximum day rates by role and seniority with volume tiers and an indexation cap, fixed price per deliverable as the default and capped T&M otherwise. Illustrative rules: direct award below €50K at or below the rate card with a written rationale; mini-competition with at least three invited from €50K to €250K; presentations or a full RFP above. Remind them thresholds must align to their delegation of authority. Staff augmentation: choose between a service contract and labelled agency work; in Germany the AÜG limits leasing the same worker to the same client to 18 consecutive months (verify with Legal). DORA: Art. 30 clauses in the framework so every call-off inherits them. Warnings at 10 and 3 minutes."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Debrief: the CIO wants her favourite firm","q":"\"Our favourite firm knows our systems. A mini-competition every time just slows my projects down.\"","b":["What does your rule allow below the direct-award threshold?","Where does speed come from: the template, or skipping competition?","Can the favourite firm stay? On what terms?","Your design: how many lots, firms per lot, what kind of rate card?"],"min":8,"format":"One room presents, the others challenge","note":"Ask one room to present its panel in 90 seconds; the other rooms challenge with the CIO's objection on the slide. Expected good answers: the favourite firm can join the panel if it competes and accepts the rate card; small or urgent work can be a documented direct award; speed comes from a ready mini-competition template in Coupa (a short RFP event sent to the lot's members, Q&A in the Message Center), not from skipping competition; DORA is done once at panel level instead of 38 times, which is a strong argument for the CIO. Pitfall: teams that put all 12 current firms on the panel to avoid a fight. Ask who would lose work under their rules and how they will tell the business owner. Mention P7 creative agencies (€7.2M, 6 agencies, no rate card) as the same logic in Marketing: a roster."},{"k":"takeaways","h":"2.3 in five lines","b":["A stream of different jobs needs a pool: not one firm, not the open market","Lots by capability, three to six firms per lot (practice)","Maximum day rates: the ceiling stops the 1,450-euro day","Mini-competition above a threshold, documented direct award below","Labour-law model and DORA clauses fixed once, in the framework"],"note":"One-minute recap. Mention the reference model in one sentence: EU Directive 2014/24 Art. 33 frameworks (direct award where all terms are fixed, mini-competition where not) and the UK CCS consultancy framework practice are public rules we use as a benchmark, not law for us. Bridge to 2.4: so far there was always a market to compete. Now the case where there is not, and where most of our biggest renewals sit."},{"k":"section","n":"2.4","h":"When there is no real competition","sub":"An RFP sent to a vendor who knows it cannot lose is theatre. Prepare a negotiation instead.","note":"Bridge, two minutes. Recall the Kelvar facts: P3 core insurance software, €11.2M a year, 9% uplift at the last renewal, renewal June 2027, 9-month notice period, auto-renewal clause, no usage data until now, an estimated 20 to 25% of named-user licences unused, two credible platforms with a migration of 18 to 24 months. Recall the distinction: sole source means no alternative exists; single source means alternatives exist but switching is slow or costly. Kelvar is single source, a weaker vendor position than it looks. Then read the provocation on the next slide aloud, in the voice of an impatient IT manager."},{"k":"discuss","h":"RFP Kelvar's competitors just for show?","q":"\"Let's send an RFP to the two alternative platforms. Kelvar will see we are serious and drop the uplift.\"","b":["What happens to our credibility if Kelvar calls the bluff?","What do the two alternatives gain from bidding for a deal we will not award?","An RFI or an architecture study instead: what is the difference?","Which facts hurt Kelvar more than a fake tender?"],"min":10,"format":"Agree / disagree, then open floor","note":"Expect some agreement: it is a common tactic. Ask a defender to argue it fully first, then open the floor. Points to land: Kelvar knows migration takes 18 to 24 months, so an RFP for June 2027 is a bluff, and a bluff that is called destroys your position (Fisher and Ury: a BATNA must be real). The alternative vendors learn that we run tenders we do not award; next time they do not bid, or bid carelessly. An RFI or architecture study is honest: it builds a credible BATNA for the next term and gives facts. The facts that hurt more: 20 to 25% unused named-user licences, a benchmark of price per user or module, uplift caps elsewhere. Also: never an auction here, because an auction with one realistic bidder is an expensive RFQ. Steele and Court: a mid-sized insurer is often a nuisance or exploitable account for a global vendor; raise attractiveness (Group volume, reference value) or reduce dependency (Chessboard: change the nature of demand)."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s2-l4","note":"Run it in 30 seconds. The best answer is the third: not for June 2027, but yes for the next term. The fourth is also right and worth praising: a threat said by Procurement while IT tells the vendor 'the renewal is a formality' is worth nothing; one voice, nobody pre-commits. 'Yes' overstates it; 'no' gives the vendor the whole next term. Then ask the timing question: with a 9-month notice period, when is the real deadline? Let them compute it: nine months before June 2027 is September 2026. Ask what that means for Nordvia and what the auto-renewal clause triggers if nobody acts. The notice date, not the expiry, is the deadline; the practitioner rule is to start 12 to 18 months before renewal (practice, not a norm)."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Practice 2.4: sole-source renewal outline","min":12,"where":"Alone or pairs · Module 2.4 in the workbook","b":["Check the illustrative shelfware value","Fill the eight boxes of the outline","Mark 'assumed' where a fact is still missing","Use Kelvar or your own sole- or single-source project"],"note":"Twelve minutes in the main room, pairs if they prefer (they can use breakout rooms on request). Correct check: €1.12M a year (illustrative: 11.2 × 50% × 20%). Walk through the eight boxes if people get stuck: usage facts; our BATNA and how we make it credible; benchmarks and should-cost; what we unbundle (licences, support, cloud subscription, services); protections for the next term (uplift caps, price holds, right to reduce, transition assistance, data portability, DORA Art. 30(3)(f) exit clauses where a critical or important function is supported); timeline back from the notice date; one voice; what we can give that is cheap for us (term, reference, timing of payment). Pitfall to watch: 'leave Kelvar by June 2027' written as the BATNA. Ask them to prove it."},{"k":"takeaways","h":"2.4 in five lines","b":["No real alternative in the horizon: negotiate, do not stage a tender","Usage facts, benchmarks and a credible BATNA create the power","The notice date, not the expiry, is the deadline","Unbundle, and protect the next term: caps, right to reduce, exit","One voice: nobody pre-commits to the vendor"],"note":"Debrief the outline in three quick questions before reading the lines: 'What is your BATNA, honestly?', 'Which protection matters most for the next term?', 'Who speaks for Nordvia, and who must stay silent?' Take one answer each. Then read the five lines. Coupa's honest role here: an RFI event to alternatives, CLM alerts on the notice date, the justification recorded in the workflow; no auction, no RFP for show. Session 4 turns this outline into a full negotiation plan and a role-play with Kelvar. Announce the break."},{"k":"statement","h":"Break · 10 minutes","p":"Back at 12:05. Find your own project's notice date, not only its expiry date.","note":"Timer on screen. The prompt prepares the strategy paper: block 5 is the timeline back from the notice date. During the break, prepare the breakout rooms for the case (teams of four, mixed entities if possible). Restart on time."},{"k":"section","n":"2.5","h":"Partnerships, the tail and the strategy paper","sub":"Two ends of the map, and one page that records the decision.","note":"About 6 minutes of input and questions, no new theory. End one, the complex deal: Nordvia P8 Northbyte, claims IT managed service, €19.4M a year, supports a critical function, in the DORA register, no tested exit plan, expiry December 2027. Ask: 'Just renew, switching is too risky: agree?' Expected discussion: that is how concentration risk grows. A multi-stage negotiated process (RFI, shortlist of three or four, dialogue workshops, BAFO) must start now, and DORA requires a pre-contract assessment and a tested exit strategy for ICT supporting critical or important functions; Solvency II may require prior notification of the supervisor for critical or important outsourcing. Legal and Compliance own the interpretation, and EIOPA's cloud guidelines were withdrawn in January 2025, so do not cite them. End two, the tail: P5 office supplies and print, €2.3M, 31 suppliers, 1,900 POs a year below 1,000 euros. Ask: who would tender 1,900 orders? Nobody. Check for a Group framework first; otherwise an RFQ on a basket (e-auction only if the basket is precise and four or five qualified bidders compete), then a punch-out catalogue. In the Group, suppliers below €50K are 86% of records and 12% of spend: channel, do not source."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Practice 2.5: your one-page strategy paper","min":12,"where":"Alone · Module 2.5 in the workbook","b":["Choose your route to market","Seven blocks, short lines: need, position, route, options rejected, timeline, risks, decision","Plan back from the notice date you found in the break","Name who must decide: business owner, and Risk and Compliance where flagged"],"note":"Individual, 12 minutes, cameras optional. This is the output of the day: say it is a first draft to take to the business owner, not a finished paper. The discipline is one page: if the route needs two pages to justify, the analysis is not finished. Check in the chat at minute 8: 'paste your route line'. Look for routes that contradict the 2.1 answers people posted earlier, and ask why they changed: often a good sign. Pitfall: block 4 (options rejected) left empty. That block is the argument against the business owner's favourite option."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Case 2: four Nordvia projects, four routes","min":15,"where":"Teams of 4 in breakout rooms · Case 2 in the workbook","b":["P1 cleaning, P2 IT consulting, P3 Kelvar, P5 office supplies and print","For each: route, number of suppliers, mechanism, term","Justify each with the matrix and the facts","Name the first step, and one thing you would never do"],"note":"Teams of four, 15 minutes; one team member records the answer in the workbook. Post the four project lines in the chat. Expected routes (facilitator guide): P1 one supplier per lot by RFP, 3 + 1 + 1; P2 panel by lot with rate card, about 3 + 1; P3 sole-source negotiation with BATNA building, term aligned to the IT roadmap; P5 Group framework check, then RFQ plus English reverse auction for a catalogue contract and punch-out, print as a separate lot. Other answers are defensible if the logic holds. What is not defensible: the same tool for all four. Warnings at 5 and 1 minutes."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Case debrief: four projects, four routes","q":"Did your team use the same tool twice? Defend it, or change it.","b":["P1 cleaning: how many suppliers, which term?","P2 consulting: lots, firms per lot, rate card?","P3 Kelvar: what is the first step this month?","P5 supplies: who checked for a Group framework first?","One thing you would never do on each project"],"min":10,"format":"Team spokespeople, then vote","note":"Each team gives its four routes in one line each; the others react with thumbs if they agree. Go project by project only where teams differ. Spend most time on the 'never' column: strong teams say never auction cleaning on price only, never auction consulting (EU Directive Art. 35 forbids e-auctions for intellectual performances in public procurement, and Bajari, McMillan and Tadelis show auctions perform poorly on complex, incompletely specified work), never RFP Kelvar for show, never tender 1,900 small orders. Timing insight to land: P1 and P3 both expire June 2027, but Kelvar's 9-month notice period makes P3 the most urgent. Ask for a final vote in the chat: which project would you start on Monday?"},{"k":"steps","h":"Fieldwork 2, before Session 3","s":[["Finish","The one-page sourcing strategy paper"],["Walk through","30 minutes with the business owner: problem, route, options rejected, timeline"],["Involve","Risk and Compliance where triage flagged outsourcing, DORA, data or staff transfer"],["Check","Any Group or entity framework, and the current notice date"],["Bring","The agreed paper to Session 3"]],"note":"Brief it clearly. They are not asking permission to run a tender; they are agreeing how the business will buy and why. Lead with the problem the business owner already feels (HQ complaints, rates that vary by 77%, a 9% uplift), then the route, then the rejected options. Prepare for the three classic pushbacks: 'we already have a favourite supplier' (the panel and direct-award rules answer it), 'a tender takes too long' (the timeline from the notice date answers it), 'the vendor will not negotiate' (the usage facts and the BATNA answer it). Session 3 designs the RFx and the evaluation model, so the route must be agreed before then. Ask everyone to type the meeting date in the chat as a commitment."},{"k":"takeaways","h":"Session 2 in five lines","b":["Analyse first, choose the strategy second, pick the tool last","Cleaning and security: one supplier, tendered about every three years","Professional and IT services: a pool of preferred suppliers with a rate card and mini-competitions","Incumbent software: a prepared sole-source negotiation, never an RFP for show or an auction","Complex deals need a multi-stage process; the tail needs a channel; all of it fits on one page"],"note":"This is also the recap of 2.5. Read the five lines slowly. Then go back to the chat answers from 2.1 ('which row is my project in?'): ask three people whether their answer changed today and why. Thank them for the debate. Announce Session 3: HOW do we run the event? RFx, evaluation and e-auctions in Coupa, including the Nordvia cleaning bids and the e-auction preconditions. They should bring their agreed strategy paper. Finish on time at 13:00."}],"polls":[{"id":"s2-l1","q":"After Fieldwork 1, what happened to the scope of your project?","options":["Confirmed as I wrote it","Narrowed or split","Grew or changed shape","The real need turned out to be something else","Conversation not held yet"]},{"id":"s2-l2","q":"Nordvia office cleaning, €2.4M a year across 3 sites. Which design would you defend in front of the Head of Real Estate?","options":["One supplier per lot, tendered for 3 + 1 + 1","Two cleaners per site, to keep them competing","A panel of cleaners, call-off per building","A yearly RFQ, lowest price wins","Roll over again and fix the complaints"]},{"id":"s2-l3","q":"Nordvia IT services panel: how many firms would you put in each lot?","options":["1: the best one","2","3 to 6","All 12 current firms","Anyone who passes onboarding"]},{"id":"s2-l4","q":"Kelvar: is \"we will move to another platform\" a credible threat?","options":["Yes: two credible platforms exist","No: migration takes 18 to 24 months","Not for June 2027, but yes for the next term","Only if IT and Finance say it with one voice"]}]}